    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Visual arts serve many important functions in human society — from practical daily uses to deep spiritual, political, and educational roles. Understanding these functions helps us appreciate why art has always been central to human civilisation.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Functions of Visual Arts</h3>\n\n<h4>A. Utilitarian (Practical) Function</h4>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Art objects that serve everyday practical needs. Examples: teacups, clothes, posters, furniture, utensils, architecture.</p>\n\n<h4>B. Aesthetic Function</h4>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Art created to provide beauty and visual pleasure. Examples: paintings hung in homes, sculptures in public spaces, decorative objects that beautify environments.</p>\n\n<h4>C. Religious Function</h4>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Art used for worship, spiritual practices, and rituals. Examples: idols and figurines, masks used in ceremonies, divination trays (Yoruba Opon Ifa), altars and shrines, stained glass in churches.</p>\n\n<h4>D. Political Function</h4>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Art used to express, display, or reinforce political power and authority. Examples: royal stools (Akan), palace sculptures, coats of arms, national monuments, propaganda posters, political cartoons.</p>\n\n<h4>E. Educational Function</h4>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Art used to teach, inform, and document. Examples: historical paintings, illustrated books, diagrams, maps, documentary photography, murals depicting historical events.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Other Functions</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Cultural/Social:</strong> Art preserves and transmits cultural heritage and social values.</li>\n  <li><strong>Economic:</strong> Art contributes to the economy through sale, tourism, and creative industries.</li>\n  <li><strong>Therapeutic:</strong> Art therapy helps people process emotions and heal from trauma.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Five main functions: Utilitarian (teacups, clothes, posters), Aesthetic (paintings, sculpture), Religious (idols, masks, divination tray), Political (stools, symbols, coats of arms), Educational (illustrated books, historical paintings). Each function has specific examples — learn them!\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Five functions of visual arts: Utilitarian (everyday use), Aesthetic (beauty), Religious (worship/ritual), Political (power/authority), Educational (teaching/documenting). One artwork can serve multiple functions simultaneously.\n</div>\n  ",
