    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Visual sense</span> in art refers to the ability to perceive and understand visual qualities — shape, colour, texture, and form — in the world around us. Developing visual sense is fundamental to becoming a skilled artist.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Basic Shapes in Objects</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">All complex objects can be broken down into basic geometric shapes or forms. This is a fundamental concept in observational drawing and design.</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Cylinder:</strong> Found in bottles, cans, tree trunks, human limbs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sphere:</strong> Found in fruits (oranges), balls, human heads.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cube/Cuboid:</strong> Found in boxes, buildings, books.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cone:</strong> Found in ice cream cones, mountains, funnels.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Example: A bottle can be broken down into a cylinder (body) + cone (neck) + circle (base).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Colour Reflections and Mixtures</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Objects do not appear in a single flat colour in reality. Colour on objects changes due to:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Light:</strong> Parts facing the light source are brighter/lighter.</li>\n  <li><strong>Shadow:</strong> Areas away from light appear darker.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reflected colour:</strong> Objects reflect colour from nearby surfaces onto each other.</li>\n  <li><strong>Nuances of colour mixture:</strong> Subtle variations in colour tone across an object's surface.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Texture</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Texture refers to the surface quality of an object — how it feels or appears to feel. There are two types:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Actual texture:</strong> Real, physical surface quality (rough bark, smooth glass, bumpy stone).</li>\n  <li><strong>Implied (visual) texture:</strong> The appearance of texture in a two-dimensional artwork through marks and patterns.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Surface Likeness</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Surface likeness refers to the unique appearance of each object's surface — how light interacts with materials like metal (reflective), cloth (absorptive), glass (transparent), and wood (grained).</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Cylinder is the basic shape found in bottles, cans, tree trunks, human limbs. Two types of texture: actual (real surface) and implied/visual (appearance in 2D art). Colour nuances = subtle colour variations across a surface due to light, shadow, and reflection.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Visual sense = perceiving shape, colour, texture. Complex objects → basic shapes (cylinder, sphere, cube, cone). Colour on objects changes with light, shadow, and reflection. Texture: actual (real surface) vs. implied (visual appearance in 2D).\n</div>\n  ",
