    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">line</span> is the most fundamental element of art. All drawings begin with lines. Understanding the types, qualities, and expressive potential of lines is essential for every artist.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Concept of Line</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A line is a mark made on a surface that has length and direction. Lines can be used to define shape, create texture, suggest movement, and express emotion. Lines are the building blocks of drawing.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Lines</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Direction/Character</th><th>Effect/Association</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Vertical</td><td>Straight up-down (90°)</td><td>Strength, height, formality, stability</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Horizontal</td><td>Straight side-to-side (0°)</td><td>Calm, rest, stability, horizon</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Diagonal</td><td>At an angle</td><td>Movement, dynamism, tension, instability</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wavy</td><td>Undulating curves</td><td>Fluidity, water, gentle movement, softness</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Spiral</td><td>Winding outward/inward from centre</td><td>Growth, energy, infinity, organic forms</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Zig-zag</td><td>Sharp back-and-forth angles</td><td>Energy, agitation, electricity, danger</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Qualities of Lines</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Thick/Thin:</strong> Thick lines appear heavy and dominant; thin lines appear delicate.</li>\n  <li><strong>Heavy/Light:</strong> Heavy lines have strong pressure; light lines are made with gentle pressure.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rigid/Flexible:</strong> Rigid lines are straight and mechanical; flexible lines are curved and organic.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Lines as Patterns in Nature</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Lines appear throughout the natural world: veins in leaves, stripes on animals (zebra, tiger), cracks in rocks, ripples in water, tree rings, bird feathers, spider webs.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Six types of lines: vertical, horizontal, diagonal, wavy, spiral, zig-zag. Six qualities: thick, thin, heavy, light, rigid, flexible. Lines in nature: leaf veins, zebra stripes, water ripples. Diagonal lines suggest movement/tension; horizontal = calm; vertical = strength.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Line = most fundamental art element. Types: vertical, horizontal, diagonal, wavy, spiral, zig-zag. Qualities: thick/thin, heavy/light, rigid/flexible. Lines create shape, texture, movement, and emotion. Found in nature and man-made environments.\n</div>\n  ",
