    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Drawing from observation trains the eye and hand to work together. Artists study and draw both the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">natural world</span> (trees, animals, plants) and the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">man-made environment</span> (buildings, machines, structures), discovering how lines create patterns and textures in both.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Drawing Woodland (Trees Without Leaves)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Drawing bare trees (without foliage) focuses attention on the structural line patterns of branches and trunk. Key observations:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>The trunk divides into progressively thinner branches.</li>\n  <li>Branch angles vary — some are vertical, others diagonal or horizontal.</li>\n  <li>The silhouette of a bare tree creates a complex, organic linear pattern.</li>\n  <li>Texture of bark: rough, ridged, and varied.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Animal Patterns</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Animals display remarkable natural line patterns used in art:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Zebra stripes:</strong> Bold black-and-white contrasting stripe patterns.</li>\n  <li><strong>Animal skeletons:</strong> The geometric structure of bones creates linear patterns.</li>\n  <li><strong>Veins in leaves:</strong> Branching networks of fine lines — like river tributaries.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fish scales, butterfly wings, feathers:</strong> Repeated decorative patterns.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Lines in Man-Made Objects</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Man-made structures demonstrate controlled, geometric linear patterns:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Railway lines:</strong> Parallel horizontal and diagonal lines with regular crossbars (sleepers).</li>\n  <li><strong>Bridges:</strong> Structural steel frameworks with triangular, diagonal, and vertical supports.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fences:</strong> Vertical posts with horizontal rails — regular geometric patterns.</li>\n  <li><strong>Steel frameworks:</strong> Grid-like geometric structures of horizontal, vertical, and diagonal members.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Woodland (bare trees) = structural line patterns of branches. Animal patterns: zebra stripes, skeletons, leaf veins. Man-made linear patterns: railway lines, bridges (triangular frameworks), fences (vertical posts + horizontal rails), steel frameworks (grids).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Drawing from nature: bare trees (branch line patterns), animal patterns (zebra stripes, skeleton lines, leaf veins). Man-made linear patterns: railway lines, bridges, fences, steel frameworks — all use geometric linear structures.\n</div>\n  ",
