    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Shading</span> is the technique of adding tonal values (light and dark areas) to a drawing to create the illusion of three-dimensionality, depth, and form. Without shading, drawings remain flat outlines.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Concept of Shading</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Shading represents how light falls on an object — creating highlights (lightest areas), mid-tones, and shadows (darkest areas). The range from light to dark is called the <strong>tonal range</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Shading Techniques</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>Hatching</strong></td><td>Parallel lines drawn close together to create tone</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Cross-hatching</strong></td><td>Two or more sets of parallel lines crossing each other to create deeper tone</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Blending/Smudging</strong></td><td>Smooth gradual transition between tones using finger or blending tool</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Stippling</strong></td><td>Creating tone through many small dots — more dots = darker tone</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Types of Shade (Tonal Values)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Highlight:</strong> The brightest area where light hits most directly.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mid-tone:</strong> The middle tonal value between highlight and shadow.</li>\n  <li><strong>Core shadow:</strong> The darkest area on the object itself.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reflected light:</strong> A lighter area just inside the shadow edge, caused by light bouncing off nearby surfaces.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cast shadow:</strong> The shadow an object throws onto adjacent surfaces.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Four shading techniques: hatching (parallel lines), cross-hatching (crossing parallel lines), blending/smudging (smooth transitions), stippling (dots). Tonal values from light to dark: highlight → mid-tone → core shadow. Cast shadow = shadow on adjacent surface.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Shading = adding tonal values to create 3D illusion. Techniques: hatching, cross-hatching, blending, stippling. Tonal scale: highlight (lightest) → mid-tone → core shadow (darkest). Cast shadow on adjacent surfaces.\n</div>\n  ",
