    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Modelling</span> is an additive sculptural process in which form is built up by adding material — most commonly clay, wax, or plaster. It is one of the most intuitive and directly expressive of all sculptural processes.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Modelling</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Modelling is the process of shaping a pliable (soft, workable) material into a desired three-dimensional form by adding, pressing, pinching, and smoothing the material. It is an additive process — material is added to build up the form.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Materials Used in Modelling</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Clay:</strong> The most common modelling material — natural (earthen) clay for fired ceramics; oil-based clay for temporary models.</li>\n  <li><strong>Wax:</strong> Used in the lost-wax casting process and for detailed jewellery modelling.</li>\n  <li><strong>Plaster of Paris:</strong> Mixed with water to create moulds or model forms — sets hard.</li>\n  <li><strong>Paper pulp (papier-mâché):</strong> Layers of paper with adhesive built over an armature.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Clay Preparation</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Wedging:</strong> Kneading clay to remove air bubbles — prevents cracking during firing.</li>\n  <li><strong>Consistency:</strong> Clay must be neither too wet (sticky) nor too dry (crumbly).</li>\n  <li><strong>Joining clay:</strong> Use slip (liquid clay) to join clay pieces together (score and slip technique).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Modelling = additive process (adding material). Main materials: clay, wax, plaster of Paris, papier-mâché. Clay preparation: wedging removes air bubbles. Joining clay = score and slip technique.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Modelling = building form by adding material. Materials: clay (most common), wax, plaster, papier-mâché. Clay prep: wedge (remove air) → appropriate consistency → join with score and slip.\n</div>\n  ",
