    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic focuses on <span class=\"learn-keyword\">animal modelling</span> in clay and further explores how artists observe and simplify natural animal forms into expressive sculptural volumes.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Modelling Animals in Clay</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Animal modelling requires the same systematic approach as human figure modelling:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Observe the animal carefully — note its basic body masses and proportions.</li>\n  <li>Identify the basic geometric forms (cylinder, sphere, cone) underlying the body.</li>\n  <li>Build an armature if the animal has extended limbs or thin legs.</li>\n  <li>Block in main body masses (torso, head, limbs) before refining.</li>\n  <li>Add surface texture (fur, scales, feathers) using modelling tools.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Basic Forms in Common Animals</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Animal</th><th>Basic Forms</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Dog/Cat</td><td>Cylinder (body) + sphere (head) + cylinders (legs) + cone (tail)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fish</td><td>Flattened oval (body) + fan shapes (fins/tail)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Bird</td><td>Egg (body) + sphere (head) + cone (beak) + flat forms (wings)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Elephant</td><td>Large cuboid/oval (body) + sphere (head) + cylinders (legs) + cylinder (trunk)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Animal modelling process: observe → identify basic forms → armature if needed → block main masses → refine → add surface texture. Know basic forms for dog (cylinder + sphere), fish (oval), bird (egg + cone beak), elephant (large oval + cylinder trunk).\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Animal modelling: observe → basic forms → armature → block masses → refine → texture. Animals are based on cylinders, spheres, ovals, cones. Surface texture added last using modelling tools.\n</div>\n  ",
