    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This final modelling topic covers <span class=\"learn-keyword\">ceramic decoration</span>, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">finishing techniques</span>, and how clay models are prepared for display or firing.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Ceramic Decoration Techniques</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>Incising</strong></td><td>Drawing patterns into soft clay using a sharp tool</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Stamping</strong></td><td>Pressing textured objects into clay to create repeated impressions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Slip decoration</strong></td><td>Applying coloured liquid clay to surfaces for decorative patterns</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Glazing</strong></td><td>Applying glass-forming liquid before firing to create coloured, sealed surface</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Burnishing</strong></td><td>Rubbing the leather-hard clay surface with a smooth tool to create a shiny finish</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Stages of Clay Drying</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Wet clay:</strong> Fully pliable — can be fully shaped.</li>\n  <li><strong>Leather-hard:</strong> Firm but still slightly moist — ideal for joining, trimming, carving surface detail.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bone dry:</strong> Completely dry but unfired — very fragile. Ready for kiln.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bisqueware:</strong> After first firing — porous, ready for glazing.</li>\n  <li><strong>Glazeware:</strong> After second firing with glaze — finished ceramic.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Decoration techniques: incising (cut into clay), stamping (press in), slip decoration (coloured liquid clay), glazing (glass coating), burnishing (polish leather-hard). Stages: wet → leather-hard → bone dry → bisqueware → glazeware.\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Decoration: incising, stamping, slip, glazing, burnishing. Drying stages: wet → leather-hard (best for joining/carving) → bone dry → bisqueware (after 1st fire) → glazeware (after 2nd fire).\n</div>\n  ",
