      {"q": "The concept of 'masquerade' in Nigerian art involves:", "o": ["Simply wearing a mask", "Holistic performance combining mask, costume, music, dance, and spiritual belief", "Professional theatrical entertainment", "Identical to Western theatre"], "a": 1, "e": "African masquerades are holistic — mask + costume + music + dance + spiritual/social purpose create an entity beyond mere performance.", "h": "Masquerade = holistic (mask + costume + music + dance + spiritual belief).", "yr": "SS1"},
      {"q": "Tiv textile weaving is characterised by:", "o": ["Plain, single-colour fabric", "Complex geometric abstract patterns", "Naturalistic floral patterns", "Photographic imagery"], "a": 1, "e": "Tiv textiles feature complex geometric abstract patterns — bold angular forms and structured arrangements, reflecting the same aesthetic as Tiv body painting.", "h": "Tiv textiles: complex geometric abstract patterns.", "yr": "SS1"},
      {"q": "The Ijaw Ekine masquerades propitiate:", "o": ["Only entertainment spirits", "Water spirits (Owu) for community wellbeing and spiritual protection", "Political governance only", "Only agricultural ceremonies"], "a": 1, "e": "Ijaw Ekine masquerades propitiate water spirits (Owu) — honouring them for community wellbeing, health, and successful fishing.", "h": "Ijaw Ekine = propitiating water spirits (Owu) for wellbeing.", "yr": "SS1"},
      {"q": "Why study both major and lesser-known Nigerian art traditions?", "o": ["Only major traditions are truly Nigerian", "To understand the full richness and diversity of Nigeria's cultural heritage", "Minor traditions studied only for academic curiosity", "Only internationally recognised traditions matter"], "a": 1, "e": "Studying all traditions reveals the full depth of Nigerian heritage — recognising all traditions affirms the cultural value of every ethnic group.", "h": "Study all traditions: reveals full Nigerian heritage; every group's art has value.", "yr": "SS1"},
      {"q": "The Urhobo people (Delta State) are known for:", "o": ["Bronze portrait heads like Benin", "Large memorial figures (edjo) for important deceased community members", "Geometric textile weaving", "Water spirit masquerades identical to Ijaw"], "a": 1, "e": "The Urhobo are known for edjo (memorial) sculptures — large figural works commemorating important deceased individuals.", "h": "Urhobo: large memorial figures (edjo).", "yr": "SS1"},
      {"q": "The variety of Nigerian masquerade traditions reflects:", "o": ["A single national tradition", "Diverse beliefs, social structures, and environments of different ethnic groups", "Only influence of neighbouring countries", "Traditions imported from Europe"], "a": 1, "e": "The diversity of Nigerian masquerades reflects each group's specific beliefs, social structures, cosmologies, and environments.", "h": "Masquerade diversity = reflects diverse beliefs and environments of each ethnic group.", "yr": "SS1"},
      {"q": "Tiv nibo body painting and Ijaw masquerades have in common that both are:", "o": ["Used for the same ceremony", "Ceremonial art forms connecting communities with spiritual forces and marking significant occasions", "Bronze casting traditions", "From the same state"], "a": 1, "e": "Despite different media, both Tiv nibo and Ijaw masquerades are ceremonial art forms used in ritual contexts to connect communities with spiritual forces.", "h": "Tiv nibo + Ijaw masquerades: ceremonial art connecting community to spiritual forces.", "yr": "SS1"},
      {"q": "Where are the Ijaw people located?", "o": ["Benue State", "Niger Delta", "Cross River State", "Kaduna State"], "a": 1, "e": "The Ijaw people inhabit the Niger Delta — a vast network of rivers, creeks, and waterways in southern Nigeria.", "h": "Ijaw people = Niger Delta.", "yr": "SS1"},
      {"q": "Cross River skin-covered masks are traditionally covered with:", "o": ["Synthetic plastic sheets", "Real animal skin (traditionally antelope skin)", "Cotton fabric", "Bronze metal sheets"], "a": 1, "e": "Cross River Ekpe masks are covered with real animal skin — traditionally antelope skin — treated and stretched over a carved wooden form.", "h": "Cross River masks: covered with real animal skin (traditionally antelope).", "yr": "SS1"}
